--- body ---
## Context

The calibration-header ruling (`spec/DECISIONS.md`, 2026-08-31) rules a hash over
the header out cross-machine, on the measured fact that X8's fp32 rows are
cross-provider compatible without being bit-identical, and admits one only as a
same-machine tripwire resting on ticket 0486's bit-determinism check.

The obvious repair is to hash something coarser than bytes: the sign bit of each
dimension, which this repository already quantizes to (ticket 0008) and which
discards exactly the low-order noise a byte hash trips on. A sign hash would be
32x smaller than the fp32 header and would answer chain identity in a comparison,
with no per-vector cosine loop.

This ticket asks whether that works. The answer for the hash is no, twice over,
and the same evidence says what to do instead.

## What is already established

Both halves come from artifacts already in the tree; neither needed a vector
embedded. `bench/sign_stability.py` derives them and
`bench/results/0499-sign-hash/sign-stability.json` is its record.

1. **The probabilistic half.** Two unit vectors at angle theta disagree on a
   coordinate's sign with probability theta/pi. At X8's worst same-chain row the
   cross-provider cosine is 0,999974, which moves 1,763 of 768 bits — so an exact
   sign hash agrees on a single vector 17,1% of the time, and a hash over the
   ruling's 64-chunk header essentially never. Hashing amplifies: one flipped bit
   anywhere in ~49 000 destroys the match.
2. **The mechanism half.** Ticket 0008 measured two dimensions over 95% one-sided
   on 93 022 real vectors, and both are dimensions the model never activates, at a
   millionth or less of the median magnitude. Their sign is float noise. A hash
   over every dimension hashes them too, so the least informative bits in the
   vector are also the ones most likely to break the identifier.

Two qualifications belong with the numbers. The artifacts store cosine to six
decimals, so a rung printed 1,0 admits 0,244 flipped bits at 768 dims and the
exact-hash question cannot be closed from summaries alone. And the theta/pi
identity is a floor rather than an estimate: an isotropic control reproduces it,
while coordinate-wise quantization on a heavy-tailed vector flips 7,76x as many
bits at the same cosine, because a coordinate near zero needs almost no error to
change sign.

## What the same evidence supports instead, and where it stops

A threshold, not a hash — and only at fp32.

Identification needs two populations, and the artifacts hold both, in different
places. The **noise floor** is the same chain read on the other arm: that is what
X8's summary rows measure, GPU against CPU at the *same* model and rung. The
**signal** is the distance to a chain that is not this one: that is inside each
cell, where the ladder scores a dtype against the fp32 rung beside it on one
provider. Reading the summary rows as different chains is the error the first pass
of this analysis made, and correcting it changes the answer rather than the
wording.

Corrected, sign distance identifies an **fp32** file for 6 of 6 models, at
31,67x the noise floor in the narrowest case and 270,52x in the widest. At the
**8-bit rungs it stops working**: only 1 of 12 cells clears even a 2:1 margin,
and 3 invert outright — the same chain on the other arm moves more bits than the
nearest different chain does, so there is nothing to threshold. That boundary is
not new; it is the cost the calibration-header ruling already accepted from the
cosine side — the device is part of the chain at the 8-bit rungs — reached
independently in sign space, which is some evidence that both readings are of the
same underlying fact.

So the proposal is narrow: a sign vector carried beside the fp32 calibration
vectors, never instead of them, as the cheap first read that fails fast on an
fp32 file. It claims nothing at 8-bit, where the ruling already says a
GPU-built and a CPU-built file do not merge.

The proposal, and the threshold it needs, are stated in `spec/DECISIONS.md` under
awaiting ratification. Nothing here changes the ratified header.

## Sequencing

Blocked by 0485 for the same reason ticket 0497 is: 0485 decides what the header
is compared on, and a threshold whose bar is set before that verdict would be set
against the wrong metric. The analysis above does not depend on it; freezing a
number does.

## Actions

1. Confirm on real vectors what the cosines only imply: sign-flip counts between
   the two providers at fp32, on the same rows X8 scored. The vectors are on the
   author's machine, beside `bench/results/0482-gpu-corrected/cells/`.
2. Measure the same-machine case ticket 0486 carries — re-embed a fixed probe
   twice, same provider and thread count — and record whether the sign bits are
   identical where the bytes may not be. That is the tripwire's actual claim.
3. Size the threshold from those two distributions rather than from the identity,
   and report the overlap if there is any.
4. Only then propose the header field, in `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.2 where the format
   lives, and put the number to the author.

## Test

- The real-vector arm must reproduce the direction of this analysis or overturn
  it; either outcome is the result. A run that reports agreement without stating
  the flip distribution has not answered the question.
- Negative control: two different chains must exceed the threshold on the same
  corpus, or the identifier identifies nothing.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Sign-flip distributions measured cross-provider and same-machine, on real
      vectors, and committed
- [ ] A threshold sized from those distributions, with its overlap stated
- [ ] The ledger entry ratified or vetoed, and `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.2 edited to
      match if it carries
